**Read:** [Calibration of aerosol instruments — Chen, Fletcher and Cheng](../sources/Aerosol-Instrument-Calibration-Chen-Fletcher-Cheng.lingua#r_03PDE8353KQ9HQKJNAHRH99NPP), the complete chapter; revisit [A documented calibration chain for a particular result](../../../inquiry/metrology/foundations/Traceability-chain.lingua#r_7XW6NVMPW7PMPJPBESW5G1757N).

A calibration relates a response to reference values under stated conditions. Traceability concerns a documented result and its chain of comparisons, not an unlimited property of an instrument. Particle size, composition, refractive properties, shape and concentration can change how a counter responds.

Preserve the calibration material, conditions, interval, uncertainty and applicable range. Then compare them with the process aerosol. A factory certificate can be valuable without settling sample-line losses, coincidence at high count rates or response to a very different material.

**Work:** Examine a published calibration and identify its reference quantities and limitations. Propose a comparison that would reveal a material-dependent response. Separate the evidence needed for flow, size and count calibration rather than calling the whole instrument “calibrated” without qualification.

